$r = (RUN 'SHOW_IT {"subject": "Two panels side by side on one image, divided down the middle. LEFT PANEL: the tattoo design alone on a plain white background, shown flat exactly as described, no body, drawn large so it fills most of the panel. RIGHT PANEL: Studio photograph of a real person''s body showing the same exact tattoo where described, healed tattoo on real skin in soft even light, the rest of the body modestly covered by simple plain clothing, only the tattooed area bare, plain seamless light grey studio backdrop - no room, no furniture, no scenery. The tattoo in both panels is identical. The tattoo: A three-dimensional cube is constructed from fine black parallel lines, but several lines escape beyond its edges. One internal plane burns warm orange, creating a surprising focal depth without overwhelming the structure. Architectural, dimensional, sharp. In the right panel it is on a man, outer forearm, using the long surface to let the escaping lines breathe. Create exceptional contemporary Geometric tattoo artwork where geometry is the primary visual language and the human body actively determines the artwork. Do not design a flat geometric image and then simply place it on the stated body part. First understand the anatomy of the stated placement including curvature, centerline, width, taper, muscle direction, joint movement, rotation and neighboring anatomical planes. Construct the artwork specifically for that surface. Shoulders should exploit the rounded deltoid. Forearms may use rotation and changing circumference. Elbows and knees may become active centers, interruptions or transformation points. Sternum and chest pieces may use the bodys central axis and bilateral structure. Spine designs may build from or deliberately challenge the spinal centerline. Calf designs should respond to taper. Thigh and hip pieces should exploit broad curved surfaces. Rib designs should travel naturally with the torso. Explore circles, lines, grids, arcs, triangles, polygons, polyhedra, radial structures, tessellations, repetition, contour systems, symmetry, controlled asymmetry, scale progression, rotation, transformation, interference patterns, optical illusion, impossible objects, architectural geometry and mathematical relationships. Sacred geometry and mandala structures may appear selectively but must not define the category. Do not repeatedly create polygon animals, connected diamonds, random triangles or generic geometric flash. Geometry may exist alone or interact meaningfully with animals, insects, flowers, trees, ocean, landscape, architecture, cosmic imagery and other subjects. When another subject appears, geometry should transform, organize, fracture, distort, reveal, contain or interact with it rather than simply sit behind it. COLOR IS ENCOURAGED THROUGHOUT THE COLLECTION. FIRST CREATE AN EXCEPTIONAL GEOMETRIC TATTOO. THEN DECIDE HOW COLOR MAKES THAT PARTICULAR ARTWORK BETTER. Use color to create depth, transparency, transformation, movement, atmosphere, hierarchy, light, separation between systems or a deliberate interruption inside otherwise black geometry. Most colorful tattoos should use controlled palettes of one to four important colors rather than every available color. Explore sophisticated combinations such as cobalt and orange, turquoise and coral, emerald and gold, burgundy and pink, violet and turquoise, red and black, or one electric color against black and natural skin. Broader spectrum color is allowed when the actual artistic concept requires it. Do not turn Geometric into rainbow geometry. Do not create endless jewel-colored diamonds, cubes, polygons or individually colored cells. Do not simply color a black geometric design after creating it. Color must participate in the concept from the beginning. Some exceptional pieces should remain black and natural skin when color would weaken the artwork. Preserve generous negative skin and sufficient spacing so complex structures remain readable. Precision is fundamental. Lines should feel deliberately aligned. Repetition should be coherent. Circles and radial systems must be intentionally designed for curved anatomy rather than accidentally distorted by it. Creativity should come from mathematical relationships, anatomy, movement, transformation, negative space, optical illusion, color, perspective, natural subjects, architecture and impossible spatial relationships. Most pieces should remain wearable small and medium tattoos. Use fewer large pieces and only rare major body compositions. THE STYLE IS GEOMETRIC. THE ART CAN GO ANYWHERE. THE BODY DETERMINES THE GEOMETRY. THE GEOMETRY REVEALS THE BODY. COLOR BRINGS THE GEOMETRY TO LIFE.", "raw": true, "aspect": "3:2"}' | Out-String)
$u = [regex]::Match($r, "https://auras\.guide/image/img_[a-z0-9]+").Value; if ($u) { "  " + $u; $out += ("Geometric #02: " + $u) } else { "  FAILED: " + $r.Substring(0,[Math]::Min(300,$r.Length)) }
